  "narrative": "No validation assessments are due at this exploration stage, so I am not changing claim statuses. The current strongest supported dataset-specific associations are H5 SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H8 TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, H9 MSI-high status with stronger WRN dependency, and H22 KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. These effects are large enough that the main scientific uncertainty is no longer whether the associations exist in this dataset, but whether their interpretation can be sharpened. I therefore register refinements that test mutual independence among the accepted genomic alterations and assay-quality robustness beyond screen ROC and CRISPR library. The first four proposals restrict each accepted effect to lines that lack the other major accepted co-alterations: SMARCA4 effects are checked among TP53-, KEAP1-, and MSI-negative lines; TP53 effects among KEAP1- and SMARCA4-intact lines; KEAP1 effects among TP53-, SMARCA4-, and MSI-negative lines; and MSI-high WRN effects among TP53-, KEAP1-, and SMARCA4-intact lines. If these remain large, the accepted effects are not easily reduced to a single broad co-alteration phenotype; if they attenuate, the effects should be interpreted more cautiously as composite genomic-state associations rather than isolated gene-marker effects. The next four proposals test all four accepted effects in the high Cas9-activity subset. Screen ROC and library checks reduce broad assay-quality concerns, but Cas9 activity more directly captures editing efficiency; dependence differences driven by noisy or low-editing screens could compress or inflate effects, whereas persistence among high-Cas9 lines would make the associations more credible within this dataset. Finally, I add a biologically motivated WRN-specific follow-up: BRCA2 loss may indicate DNA-repair or replication-stress phenotypes that could extend WRN dependency beyond MSI-high status. Testing BRCA2 loss in high-quality screens, and again in MSI-high-negative lines, evaluates whether WRN dependency is broadly DNA-repair-associated or specifically MSI-associated. All conclusions remain observational associations in the supplied lung cell-line dataset and do not imply causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted dataset-specific associations remain H5 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H8 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; H9 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; H22 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, independently validated by R26. Robustness refinements accepted/closed for adenocarcinoma and high screen ROC: H19, H24, H25, H29, H31, H32, H33, H34. H6 remains rejected/closed; original canonical KEAP1/NFE2L2 direction unsupported.\n\nPending lower-priority unresolved/deferred primary claims include H1 KRAS, H3 EGFR, H4 ERBB2, H7 MDM2, H13 KIF18A, H14 STAT3, H15 TMED10. Many subgroup/library/quality refinements are still untested, but the highest scientific priority is specificity and assay-quality independence of the four large effects.\n\nNewly registered in this explore stage: (1) mutual independence checks among accepted genomic markers for SMARCA4, TP53, KEAP1, and MSI-high WRN; (2) high Cas9-activity robustness checks for all four accepted effects; (3) BRCA2 loss -> WRN dependency in high-quality screens and specifically in MSI-high-negative lines. No validation request is being made yet. Validation will be considered in later appraise stages if H5 or H8 remains strong and specific; H9 and H22 already have very strong support, though additional specificity checks still matter."
